{% block how_it_works %}
## How it works

The A/B Transitions widget draws the edge screen of
[`get_ab_transitions`](/docs/tools/get-ab-transitions) as a matrix: it computes
nothing of its own. Its headless twin, `ab_transitions_data`, is an alias of
`get_ab_transitions` (same arguments, same frame), and every cell of the matrix
is one row of that frame: `source` is the row, `target` the column. Any number
in the widget can be reproduced, and quoted, from code.

### Reading a cell

- **The background is the delta** `group1 − group2`: red where group 1 is
  higher, blue where it is lower (the colours of the diff transition graph and
  of its significance overlay), on a scale symmetric around 0 whose full colour
  is the largest `|delta|` among the cells shown. A few large deltas wash out
  the rest: set a smaller maximum in **Colour scale maximum** to see them.
- **The text is the delta and its interval** at `ci_level`, in percentage points
  of the arm's paths for `unique_paths` and the conditional weights, in
  transitions per path for `avg_per_path`. When the cells get too small (more
  than 24 targets, or **Cell size: Compact**), the interval moves to the tooltip.
- **The wide outline is the test**, read off `p_value_adjusted`:
  - **black**: the adjusted p-value is below `alpha = 1 − ci_level`, the delta
    is significantly different from 0 after the correction;
  - **light grey with a grey cross**: tested, not significant. The data cannot
    tell this delta from 0; it is not evidence of no difference, read the
    interval for the effects it still allows;
  - **dashed light grey with a cross, muted text**: not tested (no adjusted
    p-value): too little pooled support for `min_support`, no spread to
    estimate, or bootstrap replicates without a value. The tooltip says which;
  - **an empty cell** has no row in the frame: the transition never occurs in
    the compared paths of either arm.

The tooltip carries the whole row: both arms' values, the delta and its
interval (and the simultaneous Bonferroni one when the correction is
`bonferroni`), `rel_delta`, `p_value`, `p_value_adjusted` with the correction
and the family size, the MDE, the arms' support and paths with the edge, the
support warning, `conditional`, the verdict and the row's `reading`, verbatim.
The header names the arms, the edge weight, the population (the stream's paths,
or intention to treat with `assignment=`), the correction over how many tested
edges, and `alpha`.

**The intervals are per cell, not simultaneous.** Each holds at `ci_level` for
its edge alone, while the outline reads the p-value adjusted over every tested
edge, so a grey cell's interval can exclude 0. With `correction="bonferroni"`
the frame also has simultaneous intervals; **Intervals: Simultaneous** shows
them in the cells.

### Conditional weights

`proba_out`, `proba_in` and `share_of_total` divide by behaviour the treatment
can change; their rows carry `conditional=True`. In each arm a `proba_out` row
sums to 100 %, so the deltas of a row sum to 0: read across a row, as "where
the transitions out of this source go". `proba_in` is the same down a column,
and `share_of_total` over the whole matrix, so a cell also moves when any other
transition changes. A hint above the matrix says so (**Reading hint** turns it
off). For an effect of the treatment over every user, read `unique_paths` or
`avg_per_path`.

### Sidebar and view

The sidebar edits `edge_weight`, `correction`, `ci_level` and `min_support`,
and every change recomputes the frame. The other arguments (`assignment`,
`horizon`, `path_col`, `edges`, `test`, `power`, `n_bootstrap`,
`bootstrap_weights`, `random_state`) are set in code and shown read-only.
View controls change nothing in the frame: show or hide the intervals; dim or
hide the rows and columns without a significant cell; the colour scale maximum;
sort rows and columns by name, by largest `|delta|` or by number of significant
cells; pin an event to the top, or hide it (its row and column). A `state_file`
keeps the data arguments and the view; `diff` and `assignment` are passed again
when the widget is reopened. A static export keeps the view controls and locks
the data ones.

### What the screen is for

The family is every edge with enough support, chosen after the data were
seen: the matrix is a map of where the arms' paths part and a source of
hypotheses, not a launch decision. A black cell is confirmed by declaring it
as the metric of a new test. The
[A/B Testing guide](/docs/ab-testing#which-edges-and-steps-differ-a-screen-with-fdr-control)
reads the screen on the bundled experiment, with its pitfalls.

