## `ab_transitions_data` and `get_ab_transitions`

`ab_transitions_data` and `get_ab_transitions` are the same method under two
names. The library's docs use `get_ab_transitions`, the name of the analysis
tools; `ab_transitions_data` exists so that every widget has a `<widget>_data`
twin. The full parameter and column reference is on the
[A/B Transition Screen](/docs/tools/get-ab-transitions) page.

### As a table for export

[`get_ab_transitions_table`](/docs/tools/get-ab-transitions-table) takes the
same arguments and returns the frame pivoted into the shape of the diff
transition matrix: one row per source, and for every target a group of
statistic columns (`values=`, by default the delta, its interval and the
adjusted p-value). Nothing is recomputed. `to_csv` and `to_excel` write the two
header rows, and `styled=True` returns a pandas `Styler` with this widget's
encoding (red and blue deltas, a black border around significant edges, grey
text for the others), which `to_excel` keeps:

```python
table = stream.get_ab_transitions_table(diff, assignment=assignment)
table.to_csv("edges.csv")             # pd.read_csv("edges.csv", header=[0, 1], index_col=0)
stream.get_ab_transitions_table(diff, assignment=assignment, styled=True).to_excel("edges.xlsx")
```

See also [A/B Steps](/docs/widgets/ab-steps), the same matrix per step, and the
[Transition Graph](/docs/widgets/transition-graph), which takes the frame as
`ab_transitions=` and draws the same outlines on its edges.

